  "summary": "Triangles everywhere: shelter as solved geometry, the counterculture's favorite building.",
  "description": "Buckminster Fuller's geodesic dome — a sphere built of triangulated struts, stronger and lighter as it grows — became the most influential architectural idea of postwar futurism: the 1954 Fuller dome at Milan Triennale, radar domes, Expo 67's US Pavilion, and counterculture builders like Drop City (Colorado, 1965) who zome-built domes from car tops and scrap. Fuller's rhetoric (\"spaceship earth\", synergetic geometry) made the dome an icon of rational optimism; off-grid builders, burning-man camps, and the Eden Project keep the geometry alive. The aesthetic: pure triangulated structure, visible engineering, futures drawn in great circles.",
  "culturalContext": "Fuller's dome carried rational optimism from Black Mountain College to Expo 67 and Drop City's communes; the geometry lives on in the Eden Project, Burning Man camps and off-grid building culture, and \"spaceship earth\" rhetoric keeps geodesic thinking in sustainability discourse.",
